None of these is as clean as Solace wildcard subscriptions.
```

### How does Solace avoid topic proliferation?

Solace avoids the problem by making the topic a publish-time string in a single hierarchical namespace rather than a pre-created broker object. There is no `createTopic` step for each combination. The publisher writes a richly structured topic string, and routing is entirely a subscriber concern expressed with wildcards.

Adding a new routing dimension value (new city, new type) requires no broker change. Any topic string is instantly routable because the broker's trie-based matcher handles arbitrary combinations without pre-provisioning.

```java
package com.bank.solace;

public class SolaceAvoidance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Solace: No topic proliferation ===");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Publisher uses any topic string:");
        System.out.println("  txn/IN/Mumbai/savings/large");
        System.out.println("  txn/IN/Delhi/current/small");
        System.out.println("  txn/SG/Singapore/savings/jumbo  <- new country + size!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("No topics created. No broker changes.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Subscribers just adjust patterns:");
        System.out.println("  txn/IN/>                  (all India)");
        System.out.println("  txn/SG/>                  (all Singapore -- new!)");
        System.out.println("  txn/*/*/savings/jumbo      (all jumbo savings -- new!)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Adding a new dimension value: 0 topics created");
        System.out.println("Adding a new subscriber: 0 topics created");
        System.out.println("Total broker objects: unchanged");
    }
}
```

